title: _cli_transport reports success when git dies from a signal
updated: 2026-08-09T17:53:28Z
---

`lib/App/karr/Git.pm:441` and `:450`:

    $exit = $? >> 8;
    ...
    return 1 if defined $exit && $exit == 0;

`$? >> 8` is **0** when the child was killed by a signal — `$? & 127` is never
checked. So a `git` that dies from a signal is reported as a successful
transport operation.

Reproduced with a fake `git` that does `kill -TERM $$`, on a remote that forces
the CLI fallback:

    karr create "Silently lost task"
    -> Created task 1: Silently lost task     EXIT=0
    git -C origin.git for-each-ref            -> EMPTY

Real-world triggers are not exotic: the OOM killer (SIGKILL), Ctrl-C reaching
the process group (SIGINT), SIGPIPE. In every one of those the user is told the
task was created and the remote never received it.

This is the same class as the already-fixed silent-sync-failure ticket (#31):
a return value that can only say yes. Fix: check `$? & 127` and treat a signal
death as failure, with the signal number in the error text.
